Let $K$ be a characteristic-zero field and $F$ a field of characteristic $p>0$, both in the regime of [[def-nondegenerate-root-of-unity-level-one-hecke-regime]] with the same $e$ and with $\xi_K,\xi_F$ primitive $e$-th roots, and fix the integral form $H^\Lambda_\alpha(\mathbb Z)$ of [[def-integral-form-of-the-cyclotomic-hecke-klr-block]]. For $\lambda\in RP_\alpha$ let $\widetilde D(\lambda)$ be the characteristic-zero normalized simple and let $0\ne v_\lambda\in\widetilde D(\lambda)$ be the image of the generating vector of the graded Specht module $S(\lambda)$. Then:

1. $L(\lambda):=H^\Lambda_\alpha(\mathbb Z)\,v_\lambda$ is a lattice in $\widetilde D(\lambda)$, invariant under $H^\Lambda_\alpha(\mathbb Z)$, and its $e(i)$-weight spaces are lattices in the weight spaces of $\widetilde D(\lambda)$;
2. $J(\lambda):=F\otimes_{\mathbb Z}L(\lambda)$ is a well-defined graded $H^\Lambda_\alpha(F)$-module with the same $q$-character as $\widetilde D(\lambda)$, so it is nonzero and finite-dimensional, and its class in $G_0^{\rm gr}(H^\Lambda_\alpha(F))$ is independent of the choice of the lattice;
3. the transported identification of [[prop-qg8-categorification-transports-across-the-hecke-klr-bridge]] (2) over $F$ satisfies $\Psi_{\rm can}^{\rm Hecke}([J(\lambda)])=D_\lambda$, the dual-canonical basis vector; equivalently $[J(\lambda)]=(\Psi_{\rm can}^{\rm Hecke})^{-1}(D_\lambda)$.

Choice is inherited only through the transported global-basis and duality suppliers.

In branch (CP) of the regime of [[def-nondegenerate-root-of-unity-level-one-hecke-regime]], for every $\lambda\in RP_\alpha$ and every partition $\mu$ of content $\alpha$, the graded decomposition polynomial factors as $$[S(\mu):D(\lambda)]_q=\sum_{
u\in RP_\alpha}a_{\lambda,
u}(q)\,d_{
u,\mu}(q),$$ where $a_{\lambda,
u}$ are the adjustment polynomials of [[def-graded-adjustment-polynomials-in-positive-characteristic]] and $d_{
u,\mu}$ the characteristic-independent global-basis transition polynomial of [[def-level-one-global-basis-transition-polynomials-in-the-symmetric-group-convention]]; equivalently $[J(
u)]=[D(
u)]+\sum_{\lambda\lhd
u}a_{\lambda,
u}(q)[D(\lambda)]$. In particular the characteristic-$p$ decomposition matrix is the product of the dominance-unitriangular adjustment matrix and the (characteristic-independent) matrix of global-basis coefficients, and no claim is made that the adjustment matrix is the identity. Choice is inherited only through the transported global-basis suppliers.

